# SABOTAGE: T158 (freight driver create/edit — FMCSA-guard-not-bypassable) — drops the
# `check_cdl_expiry/3` clause from `Driftwood.Policy.FmcsaDispatchGate.evaluate_gate/3`'s
# pipeline. The gate is the SOLE enforcement point `DispatchEvent.:dispatch` runs before
# assigning a driver to a load; T158 adds the first UI path (create/edit) that can change
# a driver's compliance fields, and the load-bearing claim is that editing a driver can
# never let a dispatch skip this check — only change what the check will SEE next time it
# runs. This sabotage proves the check itself is genuinely load-bearing (not dead code a
# UI-level guard could quietly duplicate around): with the CDL-expiry clause gone, a
# driver edited to `status: available` while its CDL is still expired dispatches
# successfully — the exact scenario T158's new adversarial test exercises. It ALSO flips
# the pre-existing `fmcsa_dispatch_gate_test.exs` red path (expired CDL must refuse),
# confirming this is the same enforcing layer, not a T158-local duplicate. Reverts
# byte-exact.
# APP: driftwood
# TEST_FILES: test/broker_driver_crud_test.exs test/fmcsa_dispatch_gate_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: an edit that only changes status (leaving CDL/medical expired) does NOT bypass the gate — dispatch still refuses
# MUST_FAIL: expired CDL REFUSES dispatch, no row written
